WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.950
We're here just outside of London at BGFX.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.440
Now, this company made every
White Walker, every limb,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.120
every dead body since season
four on "Game of Thrones."

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.780
We've been allowed to come
into their top-secret studio,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.410
but spoiler alert:

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.920
If you haven't seen some of
the most recent episodes,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.003
you might want to look away now.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.600
BGFX is run by Emmy winners
Sarah and Barrie Gower,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.480
who've worked in the
industry for over 20 years,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.846
creating prosthetics for

00:00:51.846 --> 00:00:55.610
"Life of Pi," "My Cousin Rachel,"
and "A Cure for Wellness."

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:59.920
I was working for various
other prosthetics designers,

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.090
and I was doing little
projects for makeup designers,

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:03.200
and I was doing things out

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.870
of a small studio at
home in our back garden.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.310
I was making bits and
pieces, then we had a call

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.900
and went for the interview
for "Game of Thrones,"

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.353
and that's where it sort of
kicked off for us really.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.490
Ju: Although we first see
the Night King in season four

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.040
in Bran's vision, it's not
until "The Door" in season six

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.433
that we see this version performed
by actor Vladimir Furdik.

00:01:26.258 --> 00:01:28.170
The whole process of
making a head like this

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.050
is probably about, maybe three weeks.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.450
We had a head cast in plaster
of paris, and then we would

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.320
sculpt the whole makeup as
one in like a plasticine.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.330
We would make molds with
fiberglass and epoxy resin,

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.960
and then we would cast the
silicone appliances from them,

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.160
so it'll be like basically
a big jigsaw piece

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.580
of various bits and pieces.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.530
Every day that the actor films,

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.950
he needs a brand-new set of appliances.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.860
Ju: This makeup weighs about 2 to 3 kilos

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.923
and takes four hours to apply.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.610
But the Night King was originally planned

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.090
to look a little different.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.631
We first sculpted a whole chest piece,

00:02:05.631 --> 00:02:06.840
which came down here,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.970
because I don't think the costume
had been decided on at the

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.290
time as well, and at one point,
he was going to be topless.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:13.550
So it would've been an
awful lot of work probably,

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.900
doing all the body artwork.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.513
Ju: One of the most

00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:21.130
recognizable characters is giant Wun Wun.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.650
Actors who play the giants
wear silicone appliances

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.750
while standing in front of a green screen.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.403
And it's platinum-based
silicone, which I think

00:02:30.403 --> 00:02:33.810
was originally created
for the medical industry.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.620
When you get it in its raw state,

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.310
it's completely translucent.
It's water-clear,

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.920
and then we pigment it
to whatever tone we need.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.010
So the softer the piece,

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.730
the easier it moves on
the skin, basically.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.500
But what's it like to
wear one of these things?

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.731
That's like putting your hand
inside of a plate of jelly.

00:02:49.731 --> 00:02:51.430
Barrie: All the pieces are really cold,

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.760
so putting it on is a bit of a shock,

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.810
but everything warms up to
the skin during the day.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.766
How do you take off
something like this? OK.

00:02:57.766 --> 00:02:59.350
If you, there we go.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:02.410
Ju: It's not just White
Walkers, Stone Men, and giants

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:06.250
who need silicone masks.
The process is also used on

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.410
fat suits, double chins, and chubby hands.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.930
The show is filled with various
wounds and infections, like

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.690
Jorah Mormont's greyscale.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.363
Remember this scene in season seven where

00:03:18.363 --> 00:03:22.543
Samwell Tarly removes all
the oozing scales one by one?

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.610
It was effectively a
lot of the same makeup,

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.490
but we had a slightly
different chest piece,

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.850
which had an underlayer of raw skin,

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:35.150
and then we had a patch, which
was preapplied over the top,

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.810
but that was all, had this sort
of integrated blood tubing,

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:40.390
sort of led out the bottom of his piece

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.950
and down his costume, and there
was myself and Paul Spiteri

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.190
behind him with just
blood tubes and pumps,

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.390
and it was kind of on action.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.590
Literally as they were peeling it away,

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.930
we had all this kind of
methylcellulose slime.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.090
Cut to me and Paul behind
him just pumping all this

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.640
stuff coming out of him on cue.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.290
Ju: Turns out the team used another

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.030
popular TV show for inspiration.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.940
We're all big fans of
"The Walking Dead" here.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.010
We're always sort of scouring online,

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.450
looking at what they've been
up to and what they're doing.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.200
But for us, it was very important that

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.420
we want to do something
quite different as well.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.717
Turns out, there's a scale
for how dead a wight looks.

00:04:16.717 --> 00:04:18.580
Well, we have super fresh wights,

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:21.290
which came from Jane
Walker's makeup department,

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.180
and they are extras which
she just face makeup

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.300
and what have you, paled down,

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.160
and they pull out bone structure
and a little bit of blood,

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.980
and then our fresh wights have
small cheekbones appliances

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.450
where we can sink the eyes back a bit,

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.813
and we say they're
about 6-months-old dead.

00:04:35.111 --> 00:04:37.430
And then we have sort of mid-decomposed,

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.450
which are heavier pieces.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.420
Top tip if you're using
fake blood on somebody,

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.180
don't use a wet wipe to wipe it off

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.615
'cause you'll stain them orange.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:49.500
Ju: Next time you're watching,
keep an eye on the wights.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.150
This is a cast of Michael Byrch,

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.999
who has played various roles
in, wait for it, 12 episodes,

00:04:54.999 --> 00:04:57.840
including that wight that
jumps out of the crate

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.440
towards Cersei in season seven.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:01.710
He's played so many parts.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.460
He's been killed off so many times.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.550
Ju: For battle sequences such
as Battle of the Bastards,

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.280
real-life extras and horses

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.660
are mixed in with loose, fake limbs.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.060
It's also these limbs that
are pinned to the wall

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.255
in a season eight, episode
one scene with Lord Ned Umber.

00:05:20.255 --> 00:05:23.340
The life expectancy of these,
if you make a load of props

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.420
for a sequence, it's
rare you'll get them back

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:27.750
and they'll be in a decent condition

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.630
because they're usually
covered in so much blood

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.200
and mud, and they're trampled on.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.320
That's like my arm hair.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:34.860
That's just human hair.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:35.693
What is this made out of?

00:05:35.693 --> 00:05:36.943
That's human hair.

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:39.570
Oh!

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:43.865
So we punch full heads of hair.
We do eyebrows, eyelashes,

00:05:43.865 --> 00:05:47.020
and it is actually natural
hair, which is sold,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.400
and we buy it in long lengths,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.000
and then the guys actually precurl it

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.280
around small lengths of doweling.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.640
An arm with that amount of hair on

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.040
would probably take a day to punch.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.620
Ju: There are also entire
trays of fake wounds.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.727
We have the frozen-lake
sequence in season seven.

00:06:05.727 --> 00:06:08.430
Sixty to 100 wights on a day,

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.280
and just usually the
nature of just how nuts

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.760
the filming process is, and
how crazy the battles are,

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.380
we rarely had continuity days
where we had to make sure

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.070
this guy looked identical each day

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.090
because he was often going to be seen,

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.027
so we were able to just
make things up as we went

00:06:24.027 --> 00:06:25.530
from day to day.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:26.540
Ju: It's incredibly light and...

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.130
Barrie: It's a bit like
jellyfish, isn't it?

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.530
Ju: Yeah, it's just like
holding a jellyfish.

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.800
So what about all of
those dead bodies we see?

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.780
They're made through a
process called lifecasting.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.050
So we'll start with capturing
a former of the actor's

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:45.730
head or body, and we'll use a
silicone lifecasting material

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:49.500
and a plaster bandage, so
if you see people with casts

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.140
on their arms with the plaster bandage,

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.640
we use that as an outer shell,

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.270
and then effectively we
have a mold of their head

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.410
which we can then pour in plaster of paris

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.060
to get a positive copy of the head.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.500
These are all our, sort
of our rogue's gallery of

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:05.540
"Game of Thrones," sort
of, over the years.

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:06.373
Ju: Is that Joffrey?

00:07:06.373 --> 00:07:08.460
'Cause obviously, he starts
bleeding out of his eyes

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.040
and goes the color of radish.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.190
So that was a bit of a
collaboration between

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:16.570
Jane Walker and her team
doing his makeup on the day,

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.360
and then it was visual FX as well.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.360
They did quite a lot in post,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.923
sort of making his eyes a little bit off.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.530
Ju: We should probably
qualify that the Arya head

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:27.610
was made for her Walder Frey mask,

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.620
and the Hound came here
for his makeup tests.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:34.680
But which adult-sized heads
are underneath these bin bags?

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.380
We're not allowed to see them yet.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.350
Aging is also a process that
is used for various characters.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:46.480
Remember that "oh, my God"
moment when Melisandre takes off

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.150
her necklace in the season six premiere?

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.340
We used a lot of reference,
I mean, old-age makeup

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:54.590
is something quite common in
the prosthetics sort of world,

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.990
so we've got so many books and books,

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:00.920
so many files we've downloaded
of elderly skin and textures

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:01.753
and what have you.

00:08:01.753 --> 00:08:04.530
We did a very exaggerated
kind of sculpture,

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.650
and it was accentuating the
nose, all of the bone structure

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.390
and what have you, and then
we had to thin her hair,

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.610
so she had a complete silicone bald cap,

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:12.940
and then she had a double,

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:16.680
who, really sweet, elderly lady,

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:17.860
I think her name was Sheila,

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:18.693
and we shot performance

00:08:18.693 --> 00:08:20.026
with her as well

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.350
walking from the bed.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:26.690
We shot her first, and then
Carice matched her movements

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.503
as well, and visual FX kind
of tied the two together.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.400
Ju: That's all we have time for.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:34.670
Our favorite bit?

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:37.990
Probably the realistic
limbs made with human hair.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.090
We won't be forgetting that anytime soon.

